  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{2}-4x+5,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x-4.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2$ and the endpoints $x=0,5$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints)\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & ={0}^{2}-4(0)+5=5 \\\\ f(2) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+5=1 \\\\ f(5) & ={5}^{2}-4(5)+5=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=1,$ so the global minimum is $1.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(5)=10,$ so the global maximum is $10.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14220202/assets/9a52ee757248f6b742af36af1e6af9e801cb8e5276b119e399b6fff36d75d0dd.png)",
